<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The launcher label. Previously `android:label="BADAN"` was hardcoded in the manifest, so the
  name under the icon could never be localized. The app's own UI is bilingual by construction, and
  the brand transliterates to a real Arabic word — leaving the home screen English-only was the
  one surface an Arabic-first product couldn't reach.
-->
<resources>
    <string name="app_name" translatable="true">BADAN</string>

    <!--
      Health Connect permission rationale copy, one string per consent family.

      Health Connect has no manifest-level per-permission rationale attribute; the rationale is
      whatever the app draws when it is launched via SHOW_PERMISSIONS_RATIONALE (Android 13 and
      below) or VIEW_PERMISSION_USAGE (14+) — both already routed to MainActivity in the
      manifest. These strings are also what the in-app explainer shows BEFORE each staged system
      prompt, so the system dialog is never the first explanation the user gets (plan §13.1/§14).

      One string per family, not one per permission, because the family is the unit the user is
      asked about. Each says what is read and what BADAN does with it, in that order, and none
      of them promises an interpretation — wellness register only (no "detect", no "diagnose",
      no asserting a state as fact). Adding a record type means editing its family's string here
      and in values-ar in the same change; a permission whose rationale still describes the old
      read set is the Play Data Safety accuracy risk in plan §17's register.
    -->
    <string name="hc_rationale_intro">BADAN reads your health data from Health Connect and keeps it on this phone, so you have one place that holds your own history. You choose what to share, one group at a time, and you can change your mind later.</string>
    <string name="hc_rationale_activity">Steps, distance, floors climbed, and energy burned — so your day can be shown as it actually happened rather than as an estimate.</string>
    <string name="hc_rationale_vitals">Heart rate, resting heart rate, heart rate variability, breathing rate, blood oxygen, and skin temperature — so BADAN can show your typical ranges and how today compares to them.</string>
    <string name="hc_rationale_sleep">Your sleep sessions and stages — so nights can be shown next to the days around them.</string>
    <string name="hc_rationale_body">Weight, body fat, lean body mass, and cardio fitness — so long-term measurements sit alongside the rest of your history instead of in a separate app.</string>
    <string name="hc_rationale_workouts">Your recorded workouts — so each session keeps its own summary and can be compared with how you slept and recovered around it.</string>
    <!-- Routes are their own family with their own toggle (plan §17): they are the one record
         that says where you were, and Health Connect asks about them one route at a time. -->
    <string name="hc_rationale_routes">The route saved with a workout you already finished. BADAN never follows where you are and never records a new route, and routes are never included in an export unless you turn them on yourself.</string>
    <!-- Reliability language is load-bearing (plan §4 rule 10 / §13.7): Android may say "usually
         within the hour" because WorkManager genuinely delivers that. It still may not say
         "every day at" or "guaranteed". -->
    <string name="hc_rationale_background">Lets BADAN pick up new readings in the background so what you see is already up to date when you open the app. Background updates usually run within the hour; opening BADAN always refreshes right away.</string>

    <!--
      EPIC-19 — launcher app shortcuts and the "Run export template" quick-settings tile.

      Short labels are kept genuinely short (the launcher truncates hard); long labels are the
      fuller phrasing shown where there is room. None of these promise a time or an outcome: a
      shortcut and a tile are immediate, user-initiated actions, so honesty here is simply not
      overstating what one tap does. The tile subtitle says plainly that it opens the export
      screen rather than exporting on its own (see RunExportTileService for why it must).
    -->
    <string name="shortcut_export_short">Export</string>
    <string name="shortcut_export_long">Export your data</string>
    <string name="shortcut_today_short">Readiness</string>
    <string name="shortcut_today_long">Today\'s readiness</string>
    <string name="shortcut_water_short">Log water</string>
    <string name="shortcut_water_long">Log water</string>
    <string name="tile_run_export_label">Run export template</string>
    <string name="tile_run_export_subtitle">Opens BADAN\'s Export</string>
    <!--
      Pre-declared for the live-workout launcher shortcut. `shortcuts.xml` does not reference it
      yet: a static shortcut also needs an action constant in `platform/shortcuts/ShortcutActions.kt`
      and a branch in `MainActivity.handleShortcutIntent`, neither of which this batch owns. The
      string is here so that task adds a shortcut, not a shortcut plus two locale files.
    -->
    <string name="shortcut_workout_short">Workout</string>
    <string name="shortcut_workout_long">Start a workout</string>

    <!--
      EPIC-33 — home-screen widget labels and descriptions, one pair per widget kind.

      The label is what the widget picker lists and what the manifest's `android:label` points at;
      the description is the sentence under it in the picker (`android:description` on
      appwidget-provider, API 31+). Both are drawn from iOS's own `configurationDisplayName` /
      `.description` for the same kind — apps/ios/BadanWidgets/*.swift — so the two platforms
      describe the same widget the same way rather than each inventing its own copy.

      Same register as everything else: a widget shows a number the app already computed. None of
      these promise an interpretation, and `meds_due` says out loud that it shows nothing until the
      user turns medications on, because a permanently empty widget with no explanation reads as
      broken.
    -->
    <string name="widget_ring_single_label">Readiness</string>
    <string name="widget_ring_single_description">Today\'s readiness at a glance.</string>
    <string name="widget_one_true_sentence_label">Today\'s sentence</string>
    <string name="widget_one_true_sentence_description">One calm sentence about how today is going.</string>
    <string name="widget_meds_due_label">Meds due</string>
    <string name="widget_meds_due_description">Medications due soon, once you turn this on in Settings.</string>
    <string name="widget_sleep_last_night_label">Sleep</string>
    <string name="widget_sleep_last_night_description">Last night\'s sleep duration and score.</string>
    <string name="widget_effort_ring_label">Effort</string>
    <string name="widget_effort_ring_description">Today\'s effort at a glance.</string>

    <!--
      Wear tile labels — the Android counterpart to iOS's watchOS complications (readiness, sleep,
      effort). Pre-declared here rather than in the `:wear` module because `:wear` does not exist
      yet (`settings.gradle.kts` still reads `include(":app")` alone); when it lands, these three
      pairs move to its own `values/` + `values-ar/` rather than being retyped from scratch, and
      whatever is left unused here gets swept out.

      A Wear tile is also where iOS's Lock Screen / StandBy accessory widgets end up on Android:
      keyguard widgets were removed from the platform after API 21, so "glanceable without opening
      the app" is the watch, not the lock screen.
    -->
    <string name="wear_tile_readiness_label">Readiness</string>
    <string name="wear_tile_sleep_label">Sleep</string>
    <string name="wear_tile_effort_label">Effort</string>

    <!--
      Live workout — the foreground-service notification channel, plus the ongoing notification the
      service must post to exist at all.

      The channel name and description are what the user reads in Android's own Settings, not in
      BADAN's UI, so the description has to explain WHY the notification cannot be dismissed —
      otherwise the only available reading is that the app is being pushy. It cannot be dismissed
      because it is the foreground service; that is the trade Android offers for being allowed to
      keep recording with the screen off.

      The notification body deliberately carries no reading. A workout notification sits on a lock
      screen, and BADAN's rule is that no health value ever appears there (see POST_NOTIFICATIONS
      in AndroidManifest.xml).
    -->
    <string name="channel_live_workout_name">Workout in progress</string>
    <string name="channel_live_workout_description">Shows an ongoing notification while BADAN is recording a workout. Android needs it in order to keep the recording running with your screen off, so it stays until the workout ends.</string>
    <string name="notification_live_workout_title">Recording your workout</string>
    <string name="notification_live_workout_text">BADAN is recording. Tap to open, or end the workout to stop.</string>
</resources>
